# Fearless Focus

This structure turns the book *Fearless Focus* into a plan you can follow: where you are now, who you want to be, what you want and why, what stands in your way, and the daily and weekly habits that keep you moving.

It builds on the built-in types. Goals, milestones, risks, controls, assessments and tasks come from Brainstorm itself, and this structure adds what is particular to the book.

## The plan

Everything in the book hangs off one plan. In the app, the plan is the top of the tree, and its kanban board shows every task in it.

```bss
t FocusPlan|ufp
    """
    Your Fearless Focus plan: where you are now, who you want to be, what you want and why,
    what stands in your way, and the daily and weekly habits that keep you moving.
    """
    s+!     Name|Plan|A name for your plan, e.g. "My Fearless Focus plan"
    s       WhoIWantToBe|Who I Want To Be|The kind of person you want to become. Your goals should move you towards this person.
    ufa     Affirmation|Focus Affirmation|The one statement you practise every day
    uvb     Vision|Vision Board|What you want your life to look like
    l:ta    Assessments|Where I Am|Snapshots of your health, relationships, money and mind, to compare as you go
    l:ufg   Goals|Goals|What you want. Your top three have priorities 1, 2 and 3.
    l:tt    Routine|Routine|Your regular sessions, such as morning planning and affirmation practice
    l:udr   DailyReviews|Daily Reviews|Your evening reviews and journal
    l:uwr   WeeklyReviews|Weekly Reviews|Your weekly planning sessions
```

## Goals

Chapters 3 to 5 are about knowing what you want, knowing why, and making sure your goals are really yours. A goal can also be laid to rest by a conscious decision (chapters 1 and 17), rather than left on the shelf.

```bss
c GoalStatus
    Active
    OnHold
    Achieved
    LaidToRest

x FocusGoal:Goal|ufg
    A goal you have made your own: you know why you want it, how it will feel, and whether you will pursue it or lay it to rest.
    GoalStatus=Active  Status|Status|Active, on hold, achieved, or laid to rest by a conscious decision
    b                  ForSomeoneElse|For Someone Else|True if you want this mainly to please or impress someone else. Consider replacing it with a goal you really want.
    s                  Feeling|How It Will Feel|How you will feel when you have achieved it. If it does not excite you, it may not be the right goal.
```

## Actions

Chapter 9 asks you to say, for every action, what outcome you expect and how you will measure it, so that you can tell whether it worked. An action is a task with those extra details. A task in a goal's actions becomes an Action as soon as it has one of them, such as `ExpectedOutcome:`.

```bss
x Action:TaskItem|uac
    An action towards a goal, with the outcome you expect, so that you can tell whether it worked.
    s!      ExpectedOutcome|Expected Outcome|What should happen if this action works
    s!      Measure|How To Measure|How you will measure the outcome
    dd!     OutcomeBy|Outcome Expected By|When you expect to see the outcome. Can be relative, e.g. +2w.
    s       ActualOutcome|Actual Outcome|What actually happened
    b       Worked|It Worked|True if the action moved you towards the goal
```

## Fears, obstacles and doubts

Chapters 13 to 16 treat fears, practical obstacles and other people's doubts all as risks, so that they can be measured and faced. If you can not rate how bad a risk would be, chapter 16 suggests working it out from how long you would suffer, compared with how long you are willing to suffer.

```bss
c RiskSource
    Fear
    Obstacle
    OthersDoubt

x FocusRisk:Risk|ufr
    A fear, obstacle or doubt that stands between you and a goal, measured so that it can be faced.
    RiskSource+!                                            Source|Source|A fear, a practical obstacle, or a doubt someone else has raised
    s                                                       WhyItMatters|Why It Would Be So Bad|Why you rated its severity as you did. Ask "really?" until you reach the real reason.
    n|0..                                                   ExpectedSuffering|Expected Suffering (months)|If you can not rate the severity: how long you expect to suffer if it happens
    n|0..                                                   AcceptableSuffering|Acceptable Suffering (months)|The longest you are willing to suffer to make your goal happen
    n=(ExpectedSuffering / AcceptableSuffering * 10)|..10   SuggestedSeverity|Suggested Severity|A guide for Severity. Over 10 means more suffering than you are willing to accept.
```

## Vision and affirmation

Chapter 6 builds a vision board you see every day, and chapter 7 merges two or three affirmations into one powerful statement.

```bss
t VisionBoard|uvb
    What you want your life to look like, kept somewhere you will see it every day.
    s+      Name|Vision|A name for your vision
    l:img   Pictures|What I See|The things, places, people and lifestyle you want in your life

t FocusAffirmation|ufa
    One short, powerful statement made from the two or three affirmations that resonate most with you.
    s+      Name|Affirmation|Your focus affirmation, short enough to remember
    l:s     Sources|Built From|The affirmations you merged to make it
```

## Daily and weekly reviews

Chapters 10 and 11 set up an evening review and a weekly planning session. Each review is a repeating item: the plan holds one original with a schedule, and a new copy is added each evening or each week for you to fill in. The guide is written by the program and can not be changed in the copies.

```bss
t DailyReview|udr
    Your evening review: what you did, how it went, how you felt and what you are grateful for.
    s+!     Name|Review|e.g. "Evening review". Each day's copy gets the date added.
    s!!     Guide|How To Review|Guidance from the program on how to do your review
    ts      Schedule|Repeats|Usually every evening
    s       Activities|What I Did|The actions you took today
    s       Progress|Progress|What moved forward today
    s       Feelings|How I Felt|Your feelings about the day and your goals
    s       Noteworthy|Noteworthy Events|Anything else worth remembering
    l:s     Gratitude|Grateful For|What you are grateful for today. Read it again tomorrow morning.
    l:s     Ideas|Ideas|Ideas to look into in the future
    l:tt    Tomorrow|For Tomorrow|Tasks to do tomorrow

t WeeklyReview|uwr
    Your weekly planning session: look back at the week, measure your progress, and plan the week ahead.
    s+!     Name|Review|e.g. "Weekly planning". Each week's copy gets the date added.
    s!!     Guide|How To Review|Guidance from the program on how to do your weekly planning
    ts      Schedule|Repeats|Usually once a week
    s       Progress|Progress|How far you got with this week's tasks, measured against last week
    s       WhatWorked|What Worked|The actions that moved you towards your goals
    s       WhatDidNot|What Did Not Work|The actions that made little or no difference
    s       HeldBack|What Held Me Back|Was it time, or fear? If it was fear, add it to your goal as a new risk.
    l:tt    ToReview|To Review Next Week|Actions whose outcomes can only be measured later
```
